{
  "raw": {
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#assistant-associate-professor-of-management-or-marketing-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Management or Marketing (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Management or Marketing (Tenure-Track). St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks applications for one tenure-track position at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank in either Management or Marketing, beginning in Fall 2027. Appointment rank will be determined by qualifications and experience. Located in the School of Business, this position will serve the College’s business programs. We seek a generalist who can teach introductory, intermediate, and advanced courses within either Management or Marketing. Candidates should identify their field and the range of courses they are prepared to teach. The College seeks a teacher-scholar committed to high-quality undergraduate education, curriculum development, and student mentorship. We welcome candidates who engage students in applied projects and connect classroom learning with current business practice. Faculty responsibilities include a 24-credit annual teaching load, student advising, curriculum development and assessment, scholarship, and service to the School and College. The successful candidate will also contribute to student recruitment and retention and help support the continued development of the School’s undergraduate and graduate programs. Required qualifications An earned doctorate in Management, Marketing, Business Administration, or a closely related field by the start of the appointment, with doctoral preparation relevant to the field in which the candidate applies. ABD candidates will be considered if they provide evidence that the doctorate will be completed before the appointment begins. Ability to teach a broad range of undergraduate courses in the candidate’s field, including introductory courses. Demonstrated commitment to effective teaching, student advising, and mentorship. Evidence of scholarly activity or potential for continued scholarship. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Candidates seeking appointment at the Associate Professor rank should demonstrate a record of teaching, scholarship, and service commensurate with that rank. Preferred qualifications Demonstrated experience teaching at the undergraduate level. Relevant professional experience in a related field. Experience with experiential and student-centered learning, curriculum development, and/or academic program assessment. The salary range for this position is $64,000–$80,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience, and includes a comprehensive benefits package. Additional professional development support is provided. Application Please submit the following materials to sbsearches@stac.edu: A cover letter identifying the field in which you are applying (Management or Marketing) and describing the range of undergraduate courses you are prepared to teach A curriculum vitae A statement of teaching philosophy Sample syllabi Three letters of recommendation Applications will be considered until the position is filled. For fullest consideration, apply by December 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#assistant-professor-of-mathematics-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Mathematics (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Mathematics (Tenure-Track). St. Thomas Aquinas College invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor of Mathematics to begin September 2027 (or earlier by mutual agreement). Located in the School of STEM, this position will play a central role in supporting mathematics education across the College, including the Mathematics major, General Education curriculum, and programs in Mathematics Education. The successful candidate will teach a broad range of undergraduate mathematics courses, with a particular emphasis on introductory and General Education mathematics, as well as upper-level courses aligned with their area of expertise. The College welcomes candidates from any mathematical specialty who demonstrate a strong commitment to undergraduate teaching and the ability to engage students with a wide range of mathematical backgrounds and career goals. Candidates with interests in applied mathematics, statistics, data science, mathematics education, or other areas that complement existing STEM programs are especially encouraged to apply. The College seeks a teacher-scholar committed to high-quality undergraduate education, curriculum development, and student mentorship. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to provide leadership in mathematics curriculum and assessment across the College and to help shape the future direction of mathematics at St. Thomas Aquinas College. Faculty responsibilities include a 24-credit teaching load per year, student advising, curriculum development and assessment, and service to the College. Opportunities exist to develop new courses, contribute to interdisciplinary STEM initiatives, and engage students in research or other scholarly and applied experiences. A PhD in Mathematics, Applied Mathematics, Statistics, Mathematics Education with substantial graduate-level mathematics preparation, or a closely related field is required by the start date of the appointment. The salary range for this position is $64,000-$66,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience, and includes a comprehensive benefits package. Additional professional development support is provided. Application materials should include: Cover letter Teaching philosophy Research or scholarly statement Curriculum vitae Sample syllabi Three letters of recommendation Materials should be sent to stem@stac.edu . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#assistant-associate-professor-of-computer-science-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Computer Science (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Computer Science (Tenure-Track). St. Thomas Aquinas College invites applications for a full-time Professor of Computer Science at either the Assistant or Associate rank, to begin September 2027 (or earlier by mutual agreement). Located in the School of STEM, this position will serve undergraduate programs in Computer Science, Data Science, and Cybersecurity. The successful candidate will teach across the Computer Science curriculum, including introductory, intermediate, and advanced courses aligned with their expertise. Candidates with interests in mathematics and other areas that complement existing STEM programs are especially encouraged to apply. The College seeks a teacher-scholar committed to high-quality undergraduate education, curriculum development, and student mentorship. A willingness to engage students in project-based learning and undergraduate research is strongly preferred. Faculty responsibilities include a 24-credit teaching load per year, student advising, curriculum development, and service. Opportunities exist to shape program direction during a period of growth and transition. A PhD in Computer Science or a closely related field is required by the start date of the appointment. The salary range for this position is $64,000–$80,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience, and includes a comprehensive benefits package. Additional professional development support is provided. Application materials should include: Cover letter Teaching philosophy Research or scholarly statement Curriculum vitae Sample syllabi Three letters of recommendation Materials should be sent to stem@stac.edu . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#assistant-professor-psychology-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Psychology (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Psychology (Tenure-Track). St. Thomas Aquinas College is seeking a dynamic and student-centered Assistant Professor of Psychology to join the School of Arts & Social Sciences. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to undergraduate teaching excellence, student mentorship, and academic engagement within a close-knit liberal arts environment. The Assistant Professor of Psychology will teach undergraduate psychology courses, advise and mentor students, contribute to curriculum development, and participate in service to the College and broader academic community. The ideal candidate will foster an engaging learning environment that supports student success and prepares students for graduate study and professional careers in psychology and related disciplines. The position will begin in September 2027. Responsibilities include but are not limited to the following: Teach undergraduate psychology courses in areas aligned with program needs Advise and mentor psychology majors and minors Maintain regular office hours and provide academic support to students Develop and revise course materials and curriculum as appropriate Participate in assessment, accreditation, and departmental initiatives Engage in scholarship, professional development, and/or research activities Contribute to recruitment and retention efforts within the Psychology program Participate in College governance, committee work, and campus activities Support the mission and values of St. Thomas Aquinas College Required Qualifications Ph.D. or Psy.D. in Psychology; ABD candidates near completion may be considered Demonstrated commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching Ability to teach a range of psychology courses, including reading/writing methods, statistics, and experimental, as well as upper-level courses in area(s) of expertise Strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills Commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment Prior college teaching experience preferred Experience with student advising, mentoring, or research supervision is preferred Application Please send the following materials electronically to Kaitlyn Pacella, St. Thomas Aquinas College at kpacella@stac.edu : a cover letter addressing the applicant’s credentials and experience as they relate to the position; resume and curriculum vitae; and names, email addresses and contact information for three professional references. Applications will be considered until the position is filled. For fullest consideration, apply by. Nov 1, 2026. Please note: Internal candidates who meet the minimum qualifications will be guaranteed an interview. Salary The salary for this position is $64,000-$66,000. When extending job offers, St. Thomas Aquinas College carefully considers factors such as (but not limited to) the role’s responsibilities, the candidate’s experience and education, essential skills, internal peer equity, budget constraints, and market trends. The provided rate reflects the College’s good faith and reasonable estimate at the time of posting."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#adjunct-faculty-positions-school-of-stem": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Positions: School of STEM",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty Positions: School of STEM. EXERCISE SCIENCE The School of Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks an adjunct faculty member to teach the following courses for the Fall 2026 semester: Introduction to Exercise Science (KIN 101), Tuesday & Thursday 1:00-2:25pm Fundamentals of Personal Wellness (KIN 110), Tuesday & Thursday 8:15-9:40am Fundamentals of Personal Wellness (KIN 110), Tuesday & Thursday 9:50-11:15am All courses will meet on our campus in Rockland County, New York. Application Process: A cover letter and C.V. addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position should be sent to Dr. Stephanie Lauer, Assistant Professor of Biology, by email to slauer@stac.edu . Salary is determined by a candidate’s highest level of education. $2,800 – $3,100 per 3 credit course. COMPUTER SCIENCE The School of Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks an adjunct faculty member to teach the following courses for the Fall 2026 semester: Computer and Information Science II (CS 250), Monday & Wednesday 1:00-2:25pm Software Engineering (CS 450), Tuesday & Thursday 9:50-11:15am Data Cleaning & Manipulation (DS 220), Monday & Wednesday 9:50-11:15am All courses will meet on our campus in Rockland County, New York. Preference will be given to in-person instruction but online/hybrid will be considered. Application Process: A cover letter and C.V. addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position should be sent to Dr. Stephanie Lauer, Assistant Professor of Biology, by email to slauer@stac.edu . Salary is determined by a candidate’s highest level of education. $2,800 – $3,100 per 3 credit course. MATHEMATICS The School of Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks adjunct faculty members to teach mathematics courses for the Fall 2026 semester: Discrete Mathematics (MTH 308), Tuesday & Thursday 11:25am-12:50pm All courses will meet on our campus in Rockland County, New York. Application Process: A cover letter and C.V. addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position should be sent to Dr. Stephanie Lauer, Assistant Professor of Biology, by email to slauer@stac.edu . Salary is determined by a candidate’s highest level of education. $2,800 – $3,100 per 3 credit course. CHEMISTRY The School of Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks an adjunct faculty member to teach CHEM 302 Instrumental Methods of Analysis Lecture and Lab for the Spring 2026 semester. The lecture portion of the class will meet on Monday and Wednesday 1:00-2:25pm and the laboratory will meet on Tuesday from 2:35-5:30pm. All courses will meet on our campus in Rockland County, New York. Application Process: A cover letter and C.V. addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position should be sent to Dr. Stephanie Lauer, Assistant Professor of Biology, by email to slauer@stac.edu . Salary is determined by a candidate’s highest level of education. $2,800 – $3,100 per 3 credit course."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#adjunct-faculty-positions-school-of-business": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Positions: School of Business",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty Positions: School of Business. The School of Business at St. Thomas Aquinas College invites applications from adjunct faculty members to teach undergraduate and/or graduate Business courses. We seek instructors who are committed to teaching, current in their disciplines, and able to connect course concepts with contemporary business practice. Undergraduate courses are taught in person at our Rockland County campus. For graduate courses, we seek doctorally qualified adjunct faculty to teach online or in a HyFlex Format. Our priority areas are: Marketing Management Economics Business Strategy Business Statistics Organizational Behavior Additional areas include: Accounting: principles, tax, auditing, advanced accounting Finance: general finance, managerial finance Management: general management; international business Application process: Please email a cover letter describing your relevant teaching and professional experience, a curriculum vitae, and the names of 3 references to Prof. C. Cahill, Professor of Business Administration and Assistant Dean of the School of Business at ccahill@stac.edu . Compensation: The full-semester rate for a three-credit course is $2,800–$3,800, based on the course level (undergraduate or graduate) and the selected candidate’s highest degree earned."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#adjunct-faculty-positions-school-of-arts-social-sciences": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Positions: School of Arts & Social Sciences",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty Positions: School of Arts & Social Sciences. Adjunct Position: Community-Based Strategies for Social Change The School of Arts & Social Sciences at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks a new adjunct faculty member to teach Community-Based Strategies for Social Change in the Master of Public Administration (MPA) Program during the Fall 2026 semester. The class will provide students with an overview of and training in contemporary community organizing practice in the United States. The course delivery mode may be hyflex (from our Rockland County, NY campus), online, or as a hybrid. In-person and synchronous online courses meet at 5:35 PM. Salary Range: $3,400 – $3,800 Application process: A cover letter and C.V. addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position should be sent to Professor Jamin R. Sewell, Esq., Director, MPA Program, School of Arts & Social Sciences, St. Thomas Aquinas College, by email to jsewell@stac.edu . ***************************************** The School of Arts & Social Sciences at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks new adjunct faculty members who are committed to teaching. Applicants are expected to help prepare each student to be an engaged, informed citizen, able to make a difference in their communities. The liberal arts and social sciences urge students to become more analytical thinkers ready for careers in fields as wide-ranging as law, criminal justice, and teaching. Availability to teach in the mornings and/or afternoons is preferred. General Application process: A cover letter addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position, curriculum vitae, and three references should be sent to Dr. Stacy Sewell, Assistant Dean of the School of Arts & Social Sciences at St. Thomas Aquinas College, by email to ssewell@stac.edu . Areas include: College Writing, Digital Content Production, English Literature, Psychology, and Visual Communications in Graphic Design. ***************************************** Psychology: Adjunct Position The School of Arts & Social Sciences at St. Thomas Aquinas College seeks a new adjunct faculty member to teach one or more psychology courses. We seek an individual who can teach courses in social/cultural psychology (social psychology, human relations, group dynamics) or clinical psychology (abnormal, abnormal child, counseling & psychotherapy) may be available. Individuals who can teach Reading and Writing About Psychology (intro level course teaching APA format and style) or Statistical Methods in Psychology (without computer software) are also encouraged to apply. The course)s) will be taught in person on our campus in Rockland County, New York. Application process: A cover letter and C.V. addressing the applicant’s credentials and experiences as they relate to the position should be sent to Dr. Stacy Sewell, Assistant Dean of the School of Arts & Social Sciences, St. Thomas Aquinas College, by email to ssewell@stac.edu ."
    },
    "https://stac.edu/offices/human-resources/employment-opportunities/faculty-positions/#adjunct-positions-school-of-education": {
      "title": "Adjunct Positions: School of Education",
      "description": "Adjunct Positions: School of Education. St. Thomas Aquinas College invites applications for adjunct positions in childhood, secondary, and special education. Applicants are required to have at least a Master’s Degree in Education (Doctorate preferred), and teaching experience. Our priority areas are: TESOL Child Psychology Children’s and Adolescent Literature Curriculum and Instruction Application process: A cover letter addressing the applicant’s credentials and experience; curriculum vitae; and three references should be sent electronically to Dr. Melissa Collucci, Associate Professor of Literacy and Assistant Dean of the School of Education at mcollucc@stac.edu ."
    }
  },
